1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's a critical engineering element in a can.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Its colors bold from the earth and its secrets created in modern chemistry.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:18,000
From caves to canvas to outer space, it's helped us make our mark.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
And it's getting smarter.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Presented to you in full color.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, paint on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:51,197 --> 00:00:53,040
Welcome to the paint shop.

8
00:00:53,041 --> 00:01:04,860
On today's work order, a pair of full-sized jet airplanes.

9
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:10,560
Obviously, no ordinary paint shop.

10
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,780
It's Goodrich Aviation's paint hangar near Seattle.

11
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:22,400
The most pressing projects today are a 737 passenger aircraft,

12
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:28,040
and this 757, a courier plane for UPS,

13
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:36,040
which will be decked out in gold, white, and of course, brown.

14
00:01:36,041 --> 00:01:38,040
We're going to use about 20 gallons of primer,

15
00:01:38,041 --> 00:01:42,040
about 30 gallons of white, about 30 gallons of gray,

16
00:01:42,041 --> 00:01:45,040
and about 20 gallons of brown.

17
00:01:45,041 --> 00:01:50,040
Total weight, if you put that on a pallet and in liquid form,

18
00:01:50,041 --> 00:01:53,040
and in liquid form it would weigh several thousand pounds.

19
00:01:53,041 --> 00:01:55,040
You would have to use a forklift to move it around.

20
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:03,040
The aircraft has already been stripped of its old paint,

21
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:07,040
given a chemical conversion coating,

22
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:11,040
sprayed with anti-corrosive epoxy primer,

23
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:14,040
clean, sanded and prepped.

24
00:02:14,041 --> 00:02:20,680
sanded and prepped. It's already had the acid etch,

25
00:02:20,681 --> 00:02:25,680
the conversion coating and the epoxy primer. Last night, we

26
00:02:25,681 --> 00:02:28,720
painted the white topcoat on the aircraft, the decorative top

27
00:02:28,721 --> 00:02:32,120
coat. We're in process right now, as you can see, of masking

28
00:02:32,121 --> 00:02:38,140
off the lines and prepping for the browns. Airplane paint must

29
00:02:38,141 --> 00:02:40,140
be mixed and remixed.

30
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:50,200
with various drying solvents and catalysts until it reaches the perfect consistency.

31
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:56,400
The crew wastes no time in getting it into five-gallon pressure pots

32
00:02:56,401 --> 00:03:00,353
and the electrostatic spray guns.

33
00:03:00,354 --> 00:03:07,180
Electrostatic means that there is a rounded charge to the

34
00:03:07,181 --> 00:03:12,708
aircraft and the gun has a generator inside the gun that produces a positive charge.

35
00:03:12,709 --> 00:03:13,409
It allows

36
00:03:13,410 --> 00:03:19,300
the paint to transfer to the aircraft instead of overspray going all over everything that's around you.

37
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:25,055
An aircraft's paint job is critical.

38
00:03:25,056 --> 00:03:28,340
Without it, the aluminum airframe would be a sitting duck

39
00:03:28,341 --> 00:03:34,260
for the ravages of the atmosphere at 30,000 feet and 500 miles per hour.

40
00:03:34,261 --> 00:03:46,380
This airplane is in 40 below zero weather at one moment, and in 15 minutes, it's on the ground in Phoenix at 115 degrees sitting on the ramp.

41
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,160
But like all paint jobs, this one is also intended to look good.

42
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:59,740
What we always say in the painter trade is that the first two coats are for dough and the third is for show.

43
00:03:59,741 --> 00:04:13,300
While paint is often the last step in projects large and small, it's hardly an afterthought.

44
00:04:13,301 --> 00:04:14,300
Paint is big science.

45
00:04:14,301 --> 00:04:18,780
The standard monomer that we use are acrylic-based, and their various structures is this.

46
00:04:18,781 --> 00:04:22,500
And big business.

47
00:04:22,501 --> 00:04:29,200
The paint industry, known in the trade as the coatings industry, is a $70 billion per year market.

48
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:36,580
The world, in case you haven't noticed, is painted.

49
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:44,840
From the lines in the road and the cables that hold it up to the lines on a canvas.

50
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:49,780
On the sea and in the air.

51
00:04:49,781 --> 00:04:56,220
on and in virtually every consumer product,

52
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,380
and in hundreds of specialized products,

53
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:03,420
such as primers, lacquers, varnishes, aerosols,

54
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:07,420
even electroplated industrial coatings.

55
00:05:11,020 --> 00:05:14,300
Paint is broadly defined as a pigmented liquid

56
00:05:14,301 --> 00:05:16,700
that dries to form a solid protective film.

57
00:05:16,701 --> 00:05:25,460
It's this transformation from liquid to solid that makes paint easy to apply and, ideally,

58
00:05:25,461 --> 00:05:26,980
hard to remove.

59
00:05:26,981 --> 00:05:32,780
There are three basic components to paint.

60
00:05:32,781 --> 00:05:36,940
You have resin, which is kind of the binder or the glue that holds everything together

61
00:05:36,941 --> 00:05:38,680
when it's finally cured.

62
00:05:38,681 --> 00:05:40,580
You have pigment, which is the colorant.

63
00:05:40,581 --> 00:05:46,460
and you have some kind of solvent which helps paint flow or smooth out as you apply it.

64
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:52,340
Pigments, which are always solid, can be anything from ground-up minerals

65
00:05:52,341 --> 00:05:57,540
to complex carbon-based chemicals called organic pigments.

66
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:02,240
The binder or resin can be anything from linseed oil

67
00:06:02,241 --> 00:06:05,580
to lengthy chains of acrylic polymers.

68
00:06:05,581 --> 00:06:12,580
And solvents can be volatile spirits or simply water.

69
00:06:12,581 --> 00:06:18,580
Modern chemistry aside, the basics of paint go back 30,000 years.

70
00:06:18,581 --> 00:06:26,580
To the limestone hills of southern France, in the beginning there was paint.

71
00:06:26,581 --> 00:06:32,820
Inside the caves of Chauvet, France,

72
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:35,600
are the earliest known examples of human beings

73
00:06:35,601 --> 00:06:37,140
painting the walls of their homes.

74
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:44,020
These well-rendered hunting scenes from 34,000 years ago

75
00:06:44,021 --> 00:06:46,140
display considerable artistic skill,

76
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:48,700
but also a keen eye for creating pigments.

77
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:51,180
They used what was available,

78
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:54,220
and they turned around and they saw

79
00:06:54,221 --> 00:06:57,100
little lumps of red rock around them,

80
00:06:57,300 --> 00:06:59,460
so they ground that up to make red pigment.

81
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:05,240
Near Chauvet and other prehistoric caves,

82
00:07:05,241 --> 00:07:08,600
the earth also yielded yellow and rust-colored ochres.

83
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:14,000
And then they also were familiar, of course, with chalk,

84
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:15,920
because that was naturally occurring,

85
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:18,300
and they were familiar with black

86
00:07:18,301 --> 00:07:20,180
because they got that from charcoal.

87
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:23,460
And that's what their pigment range was.

88
00:07:23,461 --> 00:07:27,620
It was black, white, red, yellow, orange.

89
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:34,120
These earth pigments, often mixed with a binder of animal fat to adhere to the cave wall,

90
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:36,680
are still a common source of color today.

91
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:41,440
They are extremely stable.

92
00:07:42,260 --> 00:07:46,080
You dig them up out of the ground, they're stable over geologic time.

93
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:47,920
Their color is not going to drift.

94
00:07:47,921 --> 00:07:55,760
The first major advances in paint technology came from the Egyptians, who placed tremendous

95
00:07:55,761 --> 00:07:57,640
symbolic value on vivid colors.

96
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:04,160
One of these was the first synthetic pigment, Egyptian blue.

97
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:10,440
This rare blue pigment, as vibrant today as the day it was painted, seems to have emerged

98
00:08:10,441 --> 00:08:11,980
from the glassmaking process.

99
00:08:13,080 --> 00:08:17,900
And it's not known, of course, how they discovered Egyptian blue, but the general supposition

100
00:08:17,901 --> 00:08:24,900
is that some batch of material they were using, most likely sand, contained a little cobalt pigment

101
00:08:24,901 --> 00:08:29,883
or copper pigment, and the glass that they made turned out to be blue.

102
00:08:29,884 --> 00:08:31,520
And they must have been

103
00:08:31,521 --> 00:08:34,729
amazed at this, and they used this as a pigment.

104
00:08:34,730 --> 00:08:38,340
They grind it up and suspend it as a very fine powder.

105
00:08:39,580 --> 00:08:44,720
The Egyptians also devised an ingenious method to make white and red pigments from lead.

106
00:08:44,721 --> 00:08:51,480
In mounds of fermenting animal dung, dual compartment clay pots were filled with lead

107
00:08:51,481 --> 00:08:57,860
strips and vinegar. The vinegar fumes and animal dung combined to turn the lead into

108
00:08:57,861 --> 00:09:01,730
lead carbonate, thus making the metal white.

109
00:09:01,731 --> 00:09:05,600
The lead white was then ground into a powder

110
00:09:05,601 --> 00:09:09,669
and suspended in a binder of animal glue or egg white.

111
00:09:09,670 --> 00:09:12,080
Egyptian tomb paintings began as

112
00:09:12,081 --> 00:09:14,780
raised figures meticulously carved out of stone.

113
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:17,960
These were coated with glue-like preparations

114
00:09:17,961 --> 00:09:20,180
known as gesso, or size,

115
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:22,580
over which the painters added the colors.

116
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:28,960
In the classical world,

117
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:30,860
pigments were still hard to come by,

118
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:32,900
but Greece and Rome were hardly

119
00:09:32,901 --> 00:09:34,960
the white marble worlds we often imagine.

120
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:40,520
Statues and even large public buildings

121
00:09:40,521 --> 00:09:42,100
were most likely brightly painted.

122
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:45,560
And the Roman wall paintings were stunning.

123
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:47,520
If you go to Pompeii,

124
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:50,120
where miraculously they have been preserved,

125
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:54,520
and now they've been excavated from the volcanic ash,

126
00:09:54,521 --> 00:09:58,720
you find the most amazing walls of vermilion,

127
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:02,040
which are some of the most colorful things we have till today.

128
00:10:04,020 --> 00:10:07,080
Medieval alchemists perfected synthetic vermilion

129
00:10:07,081 --> 00:10:09,300
by combining mercury and sulfur.

130
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:17,520
It became the most prized red pigment of the Middle Ages and Renaissance.

131
00:10:17,521 --> 00:10:24,520
But medieval paints still relied heavily on pigments straight out of the ground.

132
00:10:24,521 --> 00:10:26,520
Some came at great cost.

133
00:10:26,521 --> 00:10:31,520
A good blue required grinding the precious gemstone, lapis lazuli,

134
00:10:31,521 --> 00:10:37,520
and turning it into equally precious, ultramarine.

135
00:10:37,521 --> 00:10:43,220
The most common binder used during the Middle Ages was tempera, a mixture of water and egg yolk.

136
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:48,020
They would take the pigments and grind them into the egg yolk,

137
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:54,960
and anyone who's ever had their car egged knows how strong a binder egg is.

138
00:10:55,160 --> 00:10:58,380
And so it worked very well. Those paintings are still very, very beautiful.

139
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:03,600
Paint on buildings was most often thickened with lime.

140
00:11:03,601 --> 00:11:13,140
Lime is calcium carbonate powder taken from limestone, heated in a kiln, and slaked with

141
00:11:13,141 --> 00:11:13,841
water.

142
00:11:13,842 --> 00:11:21,259
It makes a good paint for masonry buildings because it allows the stones to breathe.

143
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:21,960
Lime

144
00:11:21,961 --> 00:11:26,281
paint was therefore the coating of choice for castles.

145
00:11:26,282 --> 00:11:29,820
But it would take a revolution in science

146
00:11:29,821 --> 00:11:37,300
and a renaissance in technique to bring paint into the castle that is every man's and woman's home.

147
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,800
In addition to painting with brushes of feathers and animal hair,

148
00:11:43,260 --> 00:11:48,040
prehistoric cave painters devised a kind of airbrush using a reed or hollowed-out bone

149
00:11:48,041 --> 00:11:50,780
to spray pigment mist onto the cave wall.

150
00:11:50,781 --> 00:12:01,900
Today's paint store offers a universe of color choices unknown to thousands of generations of human beings.

151
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:09,540
Customers who bring in a paint swatch walk away with a gallon of precisely color-matched paint in minutes.

152
00:12:11,780 --> 00:12:15,800
Using 12 highly concentrated pigment mixtures called Colorance,

153
00:12:15,801 --> 00:12:18,800
A paint retailer, with a little help from a computer,

154
00:12:18,801 --> 00:12:22,800
dispenses the exact combination of color into the base paint.

155
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:27,800
Out of these colors comes an infinite palette.

156
00:12:29,800 --> 00:12:32,800
This is our Flowmaster tint machine.

157
00:12:32,801 --> 00:12:34,800
This is what we make all our colors from.

158
00:12:34,801 --> 00:12:38,800
This is all the equipment, machinery, that makes it all happen.

159
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:42,800
You've got circuit boards, and you've got 12 circuit boards,

160
00:12:42,801 --> 00:12:43,800
one for each color.

161
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:47,760
Benjamin Moore pioneered custom color matching in 1983

162
00:12:47,761 --> 00:12:51,480
by developing a system that uses a spectrophotometer

163
00:12:51,481 --> 00:12:55,360
along with a computer to calculate how to match any color.

164
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:58,420
So literally, a customer can bring in a color swatch,

165
00:12:58,700 --> 00:13:01,260
show it to the spectrophotometer, do some computing,

166
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:05,180
and what will come out is a formula that tells the retailer

167
00:13:05,181 --> 00:13:06,860
exactly how to make that color.

168
00:13:08,380 --> 00:13:11,620
In the age of computer-matched, shaken-to-order colors,

169
00:13:11,621 --> 00:13:18,620
It's easy to forget the hundreds of years of innovation that led us here.

170
00:13:21,620 --> 00:13:26,620
Using a centuries-old method to transform powder into paint, Robert Gamblin demonstrates

171
00:13:26,621 --> 00:13:29,165
some of the key building blocks.

172
00:13:29,166 --> 00:13:33,620
One of them is a viscous transparent liquid that changed

173
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:37,620
the history of paint, linseed oil.

174
00:13:37,621 --> 00:13:41,620
The flax plant is where linseed oil comes from.

175
00:13:41,621 --> 00:13:44,620
The flax plant is meant so much to oil painting

176
00:13:44,621 --> 00:13:47,620
because most of the canvases for hundreds of years

177
00:13:47,621 --> 00:13:50,620
were woven out of the stalk of the flax plant,

178
00:13:50,621 --> 00:13:52,620
and the seeds which can be crushed

179
00:13:52,621 --> 00:13:54,620
and about half of the weight of that seed

180
00:13:54,621 --> 00:13:56,620
can be expressed as oil.

181
00:13:56,621 --> 00:14:01,620
Maybe 90% of the weight of that actual piece of art

182
00:14:01,621 --> 00:14:03,620
came from the flax plant.

183
00:14:03,621 --> 00:14:11,960
I can hear little pops as I do this, and with every one of those little pops, I'm taking

184
00:14:11,961 --> 00:14:17,400
an agglomerate of pigment, and I am separating them down.

185
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:20,560
This paint is starting to look very good.

186
00:14:20,561 --> 00:14:27,580
It's starting to get very nice and glossy, which is telling me that I have a nice ratio

187
00:14:27,581 --> 00:14:29,740
of oil to pigment.

188
00:14:29,741 --> 00:14:35,100
Although scattered evidence of oil painting dates back to antiquity,

189
00:14:36,140 --> 00:14:39,620
it was the 15th century Flemish master, Jan van Eyck,

190
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:42,860
who brilliantly demonstrated what oil paint could do.

191
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:48,320
There are many scientific features about oil painting

192
00:14:48,321 --> 00:14:52,660
which give the painting an entirely different aspect

193
00:14:52,661 --> 00:14:54,800
than what you get from tempera painting.

194
00:14:55,560 --> 00:15:01,280
For example, the light comes and bounces off the smooth surface

195
00:15:01,281 --> 00:15:03,260
as it does off a mirror.

196
00:15:04,140 --> 00:15:07,120
The light goes into the oil layer, the dried oil,

197
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:11,640
and the optics of it such that ray of light is focused back and forth,

198
00:15:11,641 --> 00:15:16,640
so it passes through pigment particles held in that oil layer many times.

199
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:21,540
And therefore, the colour associated with those pigment particles

200
00:15:21,541 --> 00:15:25,880
is accentuated by the many transits that the light makes.

201
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:30,380
Luminous linseed oil paint fueled the glory of the Renaissance,

202
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:35,080
and it quickly replaced tempera as the dominant binder in artists' paints.

203
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:41,660
But pigment technology didn't undergo a similar breakthrough

204
00:15:41,661 --> 00:15:43,560
until the birth of modern chemistry

205
00:15:43,561 --> 00:15:48,680
and the fires of the Industrial Revolution.

206
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:56,540
Firstly was the development of electricity.

207
00:15:56,541 --> 00:16:04,540
So you could use electricity to take chemical compounds and reduce them down to their elements,

208
00:16:04,541 --> 00:16:07,540
particular things like sodium and potassium.

209
00:16:07,541 --> 00:16:13,540
And once you've got these very reactive sodium and potassium produced by electrolysis,

210
00:16:13,541 --> 00:16:20,540
then you could use those elements to reduce any other chemical compound that you might find.

211
00:16:21,540 --> 00:16:26,540
As chemists began isolating pure elements at the turn of the 19th century,

212
00:16:26,541 --> 00:16:30,540
metallurgists took the knowledge and began fusing metals together.

213
00:16:30,541 --> 00:16:35,540
You put these materials essentially side by side, round up very fine,

214
00:16:35,541 --> 00:16:40,540
and you put them in an oven at 2,000 degrees, and after 8 to 10 hours,

215
00:16:40,541 --> 00:16:45,540
you know, close to the surface of the sun, these metals fuse together.

216
00:16:45,541 --> 00:16:52,540
together. It so happened that a number of these compounds were colored.

217
00:16:56,640 --> 00:17:01,780
As the 19th century progressed, the wonders of chemistry would reach their full flowering

218
00:17:01,781 --> 00:17:07,280
with the Impressionists, who hungrily absorbed the modern colors into their groundbreaking

219
00:17:07,281 --> 00:17:08,280
art.

220
00:17:15,540 --> 00:17:22,480
My favorite picture demonstrating this is a painting by Renoir.

221
00:17:23,320 --> 00:17:26,840
The River Seine, which is blue, obviously is made from cobalt blue.

222
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:32,200
And the orange skiff is made from chrome orange.

223
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:37,560
And those two dominant features in the painting were the new pigments,

224
00:17:37,980 --> 00:17:41,380
and that picture could really not have been painted before that period.

225
00:17:41,381 --> 00:17:51,380
In fact, we think that the Impressionist paintings are as much a product of the Industrial Revolution as the steam engine is.

226
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:59,440
These cobalts and cadmiums and chromiums were also the first industrial paints.

227
00:18:00,060 --> 00:18:06,420
This is the first time that it was possible to paint almost anything you wanted, a house, a carriage,

228
00:18:06,421 --> 00:18:11,840
because industry created the capacity to produce huge amounts of pigments,

229
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:13,300
huge amounts of binders,

230
00:18:13,480 --> 00:18:16,340
then made it possible to build your house out of wood

231
00:18:16,341 --> 00:18:18,120
and protect that wood with paint.

232
00:18:23,120 --> 00:18:24,800
Before the 19th century,

233
00:18:25,120 --> 00:18:27,440
paints on houses had been used sparingly.

234
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:31,620
And the earlier you go, the fewer houses were painted.

235
00:18:31,621 --> 00:18:35,120
In the early days of New England, for example,

236
00:18:35,121 --> 00:18:39,800
you would paint the trim and the windows typically in a house.

237
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:42,000
But even into the 19th century,

238
00:18:42,260 --> 00:18:46,220
you would see towns in which many houses may not have been painted at all.

239
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:53,040
Such is the case at the Fairbanks House.

240
00:18:53,840 --> 00:18:59,020
Built in 1636, it's the oldest surviving timber frame house in North America.

241
00:18:59,280 --> 00:19:04,100
The very earliest surfaces at the Fairbanks House had no paints on them whatsoever.

242
00:19:04,101 --> 00:19:09,100
As we move through the 17th century and into the 18th century,

243
00:19:09,101 --> 00:19:11,100
there's a period where they're relying on whitewashes.

244
00:19:13,100 --> 00:19:16,100
Whitewash is a type of lime-based paint,

245
00:19:16,101 --> 00:19:19,100
but it's a poor fit for wooden surfaces.

246
00:19:19,101 --> 00:19:23,100
The expansion and contraction of the wood pushes off the paint.

247
00:19:24,100 --> 00:19:28,100
At least one vital structure was often painted, the barn.

248
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:32,100
Most farmers used red ferrous oxide pigment.

249
00:19:32,101 --> 00:19:34,740
It's a poison to damaging moss and mold.

250
00:19:36,740 --> 00:19:39,620
Painting one's own house remained beyond the reach of most people.

251
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:46,300
But with readily available ingredients, improved transportation,

252
00:19:47,300 --> 00:19:51,680
and the invention of the resealable paint can in 1879,

253
00:19:53,260 --> 00:19:55,840
house paint companies began to proliferate.

254
00:19:57,200 --> 00:19:59,700
Meanwhile, as the 19th century came to a close,

255
00:19:59,701 --> 00:20:04,700
industry began to find uses for a different kind of oil.

256
00:20:04,701 --> 00:20:09,700
Organic chemists learned to produce vivid petroleum-based pigments.

257
00:20:09,701 --> 00:20:12,700
Now it's organic in the sense that these are carbon-based compounds.

258
00:20:12,701 --> 00:20:16,700
The source of that carbon is petroleum.

259
00:20:16,701 --> 00:20:20,700
The same hydrocarbons that accelerated the pace of technology

260
00:20:20,701 --> 00:20:25,700
could be rearranged in the lab to form intensely bright synthetic colors.

261
00:20:25,701 --> 00:20:30,260
When you think about color, most of the color that we see,

262
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:33,180
most of the really rich, beautiful color that we see,

263
00:20:33,500 --> 00:20:37,480
is coming from organic pigments or organic chemistry pigments.

264
00:20:38,120 --> 00:20:44,100
By the 1920s, organic chemistry had also led to new ways of formulating paint binders.

265
00:20:45,280 --> 00:20:49,500
Fast-drying nitrocellulose lacquer was synthesized in 1922

266
00:20:49,501 --> 00:20:53,560
and was immediately put to use by time-conscious Henry Ford.

267
00:20:53,561 --> 00:21:02,700
Before, automakers had used mainly hand brushed varnishes that took up to a month to dry.

268
00:21:02,701 --> 00:21:07,760
Organic binders called alkyds, essentially synthetic oil paints, kicked off a century

269
00:21:07,761 --> 00:21:12,200
of research into ever stronger paint films.

270
00:21:12,201 --> 00:21:19,420
And two giant innovations would finally make painting a do-it-yourself job.

271
00:21:19,421 --> 00:21:25,320
Lack's key ingredient is Lack, a resin secreted by certain insects, which is dissolved in

272
00:21:25,321 --> 00:21:27,180
an alcohol solvent.

273
00:21:27,181 --> 00:21:34,260
This is truly the golden age for do-it-yourself painting.

274
00:21:34,261 --> 00:21:41,420
To understand how we got here, we have to pop open the lid and examine two key ingredients.

275
00:21:41,421 --> 00:21:47,420
The first is a humble white pigment known as titanium dioxide, the unsung hero in the

276
00:21:47,421 --> 00:21:50,080
history of paint.

277
00:21:50,081 --> 00:21:52,900
Titanium dioxide is really critical to making modern

278
00:21:52,901 --> 00:21:53,660
paints.

279
00:21:53,661 --> 00:21:56,360
It's what makes the paint hide the surface.

280
00:21:56,361 --> 00:21:59,960
On a functional level, what titanium does is it blocks

281
00:21:59,961 --> 00:22:03,360
ultraviolet light, sunlight, from penetrating the paint to

282
00:22:03,361 --> 00:22:05,800
the surface beneath, and so it provides protection for the

283
00:22:05,801 --> 00:22:07,680
surface.

284
00:22:07,681 --> 00:22:12,740
In fact, TiO2 is so effective at blocking damaging light,

285
00:22:12,741 --> 00:22:17,900
and so safe that it's used in sunscreen.

286
00:22:17,901 --> 00:22:21,780
Before TiO2 came along, artists and house painters alike

287
00:22:21,781 --> 00:22:28,500
relied on one main source for white pigment, lead.

288
00:22:28,501 --> 00:22:31,160
The heavy metal makes an excellent white,

289
00:22:31,161 --> 00:22:34,800
but its toxicity was recognized by the 1950s.

290
00:22:34,801 --> 00:22:37,160
This lead poisoning of young children

291
00:22:37,161 --> 00:22:39,120
is a very serious problem.

292
00:22:39,121 --> 00:22:41,180
Lead is a neurotoxin.

293
00:22:41,181 --> 00:22:45,460
It affects the nervous system and is particularly serious for very young children.

294
00:22:46,460 --> 00:22:48,640
And it was banned in 1978.

295
00:22:52,900 --> 00:22:55,640
Titanium is not only safe, it's abundant.

296
00:22:57,260 --> 00:23:01,380
Like all mineral pigments, it worked its way into the paint store from the ground up.

297
00:23:03,720 --> 00:23:05,560
TiO2, titanium.

298
00:23:11,180 --> 00:23:18,180
This floating dredge operation in Florida extracts 30,000 gallons of water and sand per minute.

299
00:23:18,181 --> 00:23:23,180
Right now I've got on the line approximately 2,300 tons.

300
00:23:23,181 --> 00:23:30,180
We chew up the base of the bank with a cutter head and we've got a big suction pipe.

301
00:23:30,181 --> 00:23:37,180
The pump sucks the sand through the suction and pumps it over to our wet mill.

302
00:23:41,180 --> 00:23:46,220
The mill sifts out the titanium, which is only 2% of the material.

303
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:59,340
At a plant operated by DuPont, the world's largest titanium dioxide producer, the TiO2 is cooked in gigantic furnaces.

304
00:24:01,440 --> 00:24:06,740
The extreme heat and a chlorination process turn the metal white.

305
00:24:06,741 --> 00:24:11,240
In this case, it emerges as a thick white slurry.

306
00:24:13,880 --> 00:24:20,180
A major destination for TiO2, places like the Benjamin Moore Paint Factory in Newark, New Jersey.

307
00:24:21,260 --> 00:24:26,180
A facility like this can make about 50,000 gallons maximum in one day.

308
00:24:27,760 --> 00:24:34,860
It all begins when these 2,000-pound sacks of dry titanium dioxide are mixed into an agitator tank

309
00:24:34,861 --> 00:24:37,220
with a number of dry and wet ingredients.

310
00:24:39,400 --> 00:24:42,280
The most important wet ingredients are water

311
00:24:42,281 --> 00:24:44,680
and a second breakthrough paint technology,

312
00:24:45,940 --> 00:24:46,780
latex resin.

313
00:24:49,840 --> 00:24:52,240
The latex paint was really a huge innovation

314
00:24:52,241 --> 00:24:55,200
that occurred largely after World War II.

315
00:24:56,640 --> 00:25:00,100
Latex probably came out of the search for a synthetic rubber,

316
00:25:00,840 --> 00:25:02,400
and during World War II,

317
00:25:02,401 --> 00:25:07,120
They were working with materials like styrene butadiene to make synthetic rubber.

318
00:25:07,300 --> 00:25:10,500
They made this in a water system so that it was an emulsion,

319
00:25:11,020 --> 00:25:13,880
and they realized they could actually form a film with this,

320
00:25:14,300 --> 00:25:17,500
and therefore it evolved into what we now have as latex paints.

321
00:25:17,700 --> 00:25:27,140
I think the invention of latex paint was probably the single most important breakthrough in the history of paint, even.

322
00:25:27,141 --> 00:25:32,380
Prior to that, the kind of paints that we used were solvent-based paints,

323
00:25:32,381 --> 00:25:38,600
alkids typically, that contained a lot of hydrocarbon solvents like mineral spirits.

324
00:25:38,601 --> 00:25:42,740
And although these paints work quite well and are still used today,

325
00:25:42,741 --> 00:25:47,780
there were hazards of flammability, there were considerable odor problems.

326
00:25:47,781 --> 00:25:50,900
Most people probably know these paints take quite a long time to dry.

327
00:25:50,901 --> 00:25:57,140
First sold in 1947, Latex launched the postwar do-it-yourself painting

328
00:25:57,141 --> 00:25:57,881
movement.

329
00:25:57,882 --> 00:26:05,040
Latex resins are composed of petroleum-derived monomers formed into polymers in a water

330
00:26:05,041 --> 00:26:07,580
dispersion.

331
00:26:07,581 --> 00:26:17,100
Latex paint dries by the evaporation of water and then the coalescing of the individual

332
00:26:17,101 --> 00:26:23,480
latex particles. As the water evaporates, they move closer and closer together, and

333
00:26:23,481 --> 00:26:25,520
when they find each other, so to speak,

334
00:26:25,660 --> 00:26:29,220
they merge or fuse into a coherent film.

335
00:26:31,600 --> 00:26:33,140
Back at the Newark plant,

336
00:26:33,740 --> 00:26:37,340
the TiO2, water, and latex resin are becoming one.

337
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:44,320
This is a 10,000-gallon holding tank.

338
00:26:44,420 --> 00:26:47,140
The paint that we mixed earlier, we pumped it in here.

339
00:26:47,141 --> 00:26:50,120
We added the titanium dioxide flurry.

340
00:26:50,121 --> 00:26:53,880
We added latex, some other ingredients to finish the paint off.

341
00:26:54,220 --> 00:26:57,400
This agitator will keep it mixing while we do the quality check.

342
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:08,200
After being strained, a product that can now officially be called paint flows down into the filling lines,

343
00:27:08,780 --> 00:27:11,760
where it's sealed in one-gallon cans.

344
00:27:14,860 --> 00:27:18,200
A much more colorful operation is taking place in this room,

345
00:27:18,201 --> 00:27:21,580
where Benjamin Moore manufactures its own colorants.

346
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:29,220
Deep iron oxides and vivid synthetics are mixed in 12 color-coded vats,

347
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:36,360
which happen to match precisely the 12 computer-controlled canisters back at the retail store.

348
00:27:39,700 --> 00:27:41,860
Today is Magenta Day.

349
00:27:43,080 --> 00:27:47,240
After an initial mix, the pigment particles must be broken down further

350
00:27:47,241 --> 00:27:48,500
to maximize their color.

351
00:27:49,820 --> 00:27:52,020
So what we're doing, we're pumping out of this tank,

352
00:27:52,440 --> 00:27:55,760
through this pipe right here, up into this chamber.

353
00:27:56,420 --> 00:27:59,740
Now inside this chamber, there's a big shaft that's spinning,

354
00:28:00,300 --> 00:28:04,020
and there are a number of these agitators inside there

355
00:28:04,021 --> 00:28:05,640
that are spinning around very fast.

356
00:28:06,600 --> 00:28:10,000
There are also in that chamber these beads,

357
00:28:10,440 --> 00:28:13,320
and these are high-density ceramic beads,

358
00:28:13,620 --> 00:28:15,920
and they are smashing against those pigment particles,

359
00:28:15,921 --> 00:28:17,000
breaking them down.

360
00:28:17,480 --> 00:28:19,900
If we don't break those pigment particles down,

361
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:21,420
we don't get the color efficiency.

362
00:28:21,860 --> 00:28:24,640
So we use the mill to extract all of the power

363
00:28:24,641 --> 00:28:25,880
out of that magenta pigment.

364
00:28:30,140 --> 00:28:32,920
Each colorant flows into one of 12 pipes

365
00:28:32,921 --> 00:28:35,700
and into a can.

366
00:28:42,340 --> 00:28:44,160
Once shipped to the retail stores,

367
00:28:44,161 --> 00:28:49,360
these highly concentrated, much smaller cans will provide the punch needed to turn millions

368
00:28:49,361 --> 00:28:57,760
of gallons of titanium dioxide white paint into infinitely colored living rooms, bedrooms,

369
00:28:59,040 --> 00:29:07,680
and offices. House paints are the largest segment of the coatings market in part

370
00:29:07,681 --> 00:29:11,540
because it's become so easy to buy and apply ready-made paint.

371
00:29:13,100 --> 00:29:15,620
Other paint jobs remain difficult.

372
00:29:16,400 --> 00:29:19,880
But in the high-tech world of automotive and industrial coatings,

373
00:29:20,260 --> 00:29:22,420
the results can be spectacular.

374
00:29:24,860 --> 00:29:26,560
According to the federal government,

375
00:29:26,561 --> 00:29:30,200
25% of American homes, over 24 million,

376
00:29:30,920 --> 00:29:33,700
still contain significant hazards from lead paint.

377
00:29:33,701 --> 00:29:45,120
One of the military's best kept secrets is a paint, namely the cloaking technology in

378
00:29:45,121 --> 00:29:53,560
the black paint of the stealth bomber, which prevents the reflection of radar waves.

379
00:29:53,561 --> 00:30:03,960
While not all paint is quite so secret, the world of industrial, automotive, and aviation

380
00:30:03,961 --> 00:30:07,500
coatings is largely hidden.

381
00:30:07,501 --> 00:30:13,020
And yet, its results are everywhere.

382
00:30:13,021 --> 00:30:20,200
Wherever there's key infrastructure, there's industrial paint, protecting bridges and roadways,

383
00:30:20,201 --> 00:30:23,660
Airplanes and ships, pipelines and storage tanks.

384
00:30:24,780 --> 00:30:25,620
Their common enemy?

385
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:28,440
Corrosion.

386
00:30:29,140 --> 00:30:36,220
A corrosion reaction is essentially an electrochemical reaction between two materials.

387
00:30:37,600 --> 00:30:41,380
The most widespread reaction is when the iron in a steel structure

388
00:30:41,381 --> 00:30:47,960
is turned into iron oxide, or rust, by water, salt, and ultraviolet radiation.

389
00:30:50,200 --> 00:30:58,860
The electrochemical reaction between the iron and the various elements causes electrons in the structure to flee, not unlike a battery draining.

390
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:05,060
A zinc-rich pigment is often the solution for protecting steel.

391
00:31:05,600 --> 00:31:15,820
We put materials into the coating that will restrict the flow of electrons so that battery can't operate and therefore the material can't corrode.

392
00:31:15,821 --> 00:31:22,940
Creating a paint that will protect an automobile is one of the greatest challenges for the

393
00:31:22,941 --> 00:31:26,500
coatings industry.

394
00:31:26,501 --> 00:31:38,060
After all, what other consumer paint job must resist sun, ice, rain, gravel, and abrasion

395
00:31:38,061 --> 00:31:41,940
for a decade or more?

396
00:31:41,941 --> 00:31:46,280
its resale value and look cool while doing it.

397
00:31:46,281 --> 00:31:50,120
The paint finish and the color really have to survive a lot.

398
00:31:50,121 --> 00:31:53,920
And so we use what we call high-performance pigments.

399
00:31:53,921 --> 00:31:56,560
And high-performance really means that they're pigments

400
00:31:56,561 --> 00:31:59,700
that are designed to be durable over many years.

401
00:32:04,220 --> 00:32:07,800
At BASF's Detroit Research Facility,

402
00:32:07,801 --> 00:32:11,940
creating a beautiful paint job that will last is an obsession.

403
00:32:11,941 --> 00:32:18,420
Paint samples here undergo punishing tests.

404
00:32:18,421 --> 00:32:25,340
They experience wet, salty weather conditions in weatherometers and are pummeled by gravel

405
00:32:25,341 --> 00:32:30,740
in, you guessed it, gravelometers.

406
00:32:30,741 --> 00:32:37,660
UV chambers simulate months of harsh sunlight.

407
00:32:37,661 --> 00:32:40,640
All the while, scientists and a very expensive robot

408
00:32:40,641 --> 00:32:44,060
analyze each paint's durability and color fastness.

409
00:32:50,140 --> 00:32:54,600
I don't know if people realize that the thickness of their paint on the car

410
00:32:54,601 --> 00:32:57,540
is really only about as thick as a sheet of paper.

411
00:32:58,080 --> 00:33:01,820
And that thickness needs to provide all that protection for that car

412
00:33:01,821 --> 00:33:06,840
and keep it shiny and the customer happy for 10 years.

413
00:33:07,660 --> 00:33:17,800
Along the assembly lines of automakers, creating this paper-thin armor begins with a phosphate conversion coating.

414
00:33:18,800 --> 00:33:24,340
The metal substrate is chemically altered to make it more adherent to the coming layers of primer and paint.

415
00:33:24,880 --> 00:33:29,940
The first primer is an electro-deposition primer, commonly called electrocoat.

416
00:33:29,941 --> 00:33:35,880
and in that coating, the car body is actually dipped into a huge tank,

417
00:33:36,020 --> 00:33:37,980
about the size of a swimming pool, actually.

418
00:33:38,940 --> 00:33:42,800
It's called electrocoat because while in the primer bath,

419
00:33:43,160 --> 00:33:45,180
the car body is given a negative charge.

420
00:33:46,080 --> 00:33:50,880
The positively charged primer particles flow onto the car's surface, where they stick.

421
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:55,960
Then the primer is baked on,

422
00:33:55,961 --> 00:34:00,000
triggering a crucial chemical reaction known as crosslinking.

423
00:34:00,740 --> 00:34:06,320
Crosslinking is a reaction between two different polymer strands, if you will.

424
00:34:06,920 --> 00:34:11,160
And what happens is you build up a polymeric superstructure, a bigger molecule,

425
00:34:11,480 --> 00:34:15,580
and these can be linear or they can actually be branched into three dimensions.

426
00:34:16,400 --> 00:34:21,140
And one way to catalyze those reactions to get this chemistry going,

427
00:34:21,260 --> 00:34:25,480
this bond breaking and bond formation, is to put energy into the system.

428
00:34:25,481 --> 00:34:28,240
And one way to put energy in the system is to raise the temperature.

429
00:34:29,820 --> 00:34:35,560
The next step would be to go to a spray prime, which probably has some color,

430
00:34:36,020 --> 00:34:38,700
and the car would move on to a base coat color.

431
00:34:39,180 --> 00:34:41,160
And that's really where the color comes from.

432
00:34:43,120 --> 00:34:46,980
At this point, the car's distinctive clear coat is also sprayed on.

433
00:34:48,380 --> 00:34:51,380
In essence, clear coat is the resin component.

434
00:34:51,381 --> 00:34:57,420
It gives the color a little bit more depth, and it's become the standard of the industry, really,

435
00:34:57,560 --> 00:35:03,200
so that when people see a car, they really think that it should have that wet look, that clear-coated look,

436
00:35:03,260 --> 00:35:04,640
and that's what clear coat does.

437
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:12,680
Since the 1950s, automotive paints have largely moved from algod resins to acrylics,

438
00:35:13,400 --> 00:35:14,860
basically a form of latex.

439
00:35:14,861 --> 00:35:24,940
in so doing car paint makers have greatly reduced the amount of VOCs

440
00:35:24,941 --> 00:35:32,760
volatile organic compounds in the solvent when VOCs evaporate they bond with nitrous

441
00:35:32,761 --> 00:35:42,040
oxides in the atmosphere to form smog today the most common solvent is water if you look back to

442
00:35:42,041 --> 00:35:47,180
When paints were first used, when Henry Ford was painting cars on his assembly line,

443
00:35:47,181 --> 00:35:53,660
he was probably using a paint that had 20% solid paint in it and about 80% solvent.

444
00:35:54,320 --> 00:35:58,040
And that trend has been changed.

445
00:35:58,260 --> 00:36:03,120
And now we're making paints, in the case of the electro-deposition primer,

446
00:36:03,480 --> 00:36:05,900
you have 1% solvent or less in that.

447
00:36:08,280 --> 00:36:11,020
Though less glamorous than the sheen of a sports car,

448
00:36:11,860 --> 00:36:15,540
Other industrial coatings are just as important in sustaining a high-tech world.

449
00:36:16,840 --> 00:36:20,920
People may not realize that a lot of the modern electronic equipment

450
00:36:20,921 --> 00:36:24,240
that's largely made out of plastics has to be painted

451
00:36:24,241 --> 00:36:26,760
because they have a lot of delicate circuitry inside.

452
00:36:27,840 --> 00:36:33,200
And this could be driven haywire, in essence, by extraneous electromagnetic radiation.

453
00:36:33,760 --> 00:36:35,700
We have a whole class of coatings.

454
00:36:35,820 --> 00:36:38,400
They're called EMI-RFI shielding coatings.

455
00:36:38,401 --> 00:36:42,460
And that stands for electromagnetic interference and radio frequency interference.

456
00:36:43,620 --> 00:36:47,000
And these paints contain a large amount of metal.

457
00:36:47,980 --> 00:36:53,860
The solution is you paint the plastic with a paint that simulates the function of the metal.

458
00:36:57,060 --> 00:37:01,200
Paint's protective coating helps keep products lightweight and affordable.

459
00:37:02,360 --> 00:37:04,340
Stoves need paints that are heat resistant.

460
00:37:04,341 --> 00:37:11,340
Metal-clad buildings need coatings to battle against UV damage.

461
00:37:11,341 --> 00:37:17,340
Even welding steel can be given a coating that conducts electricity.

462
00:37:17,341 --> 00:37:27,340
And in the ultimate tribute to the ubiquity of paint, industrial coatings are even used to paint the inside of paint cans.

463
00:37:27,341 --> 00:37:38,220
Since paint is everywhere, its chemical makeup must be closely regulated.

464
00:37:38,221 --> 00:37:43,100
Today's scientists are constantly striving to reduce paint's environmental footprint,

465
00:37:43,101 --> 00:37:49,380
even as the technology heads into outer space.

466
00:37:49,381 --> 00:37:53,220
In automotive coatings, the cross-linking process can actually turn the polymers in

467
00:37:53,221 --> 00:37:57,020
the paint film into one giant molecule on the car's surface.

468
00:37:59,620 --> 00:38:02,680
As on Earth, the main job of paint in space

469
00:38:02,681 --> 00:38:05,620
is to protect a valuable object from the elements.

470
00:38:07,920 --> 00:38:11,460
Paint serves a critical function in extending the mission life of space stations,

471
00:38:13,040 --> 00:38:19,020
orbiting telescopes, and most importantly for 21st century Earthlings, satellites.

472
00:38:20,500 --> 00:38:21,240
Okay, ready?

473
00:38:21,241 --> 00:38:28,740
Here at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base, paint samples are zapped with UV radiation, equivalent to that found in space,

474
00:38:29,160 --> 00:38:36,720
and blasts from proton and electron guns to make sure they're up to the task.

475
00:38:38,960 --> 00:38:45,020
The job of the coatings on a satellite is designed to help control the temperature of the electronics.

476
00:38:45,860 --> 00:38:48,380
The electronics are generating a lot of power.

477
00:38:48,381 --> 00:38:55,720
They generate a lot of waste heat, and we need to be able to get rid of that heat by wicking it out to the exteriors of the satellite.

478
00:38:57,420 --> 00:39:04,280
On the exterior of a spacecraft, white-painted surfaces are designed to reflect the intense UV solar radiation.

479
00:39:06,100 --> 00:39:08,920
Most satellites use a form of zinc oxide coating,

480
00:39:10,880 --> 00:39:14,460
an earth pigment not terribly unlike those used by the very first painters.

481
00:39:14,461 --> 00:39:20,460
The paint also has to be semi-conductive.

482
00:39:20,461 --> 00:39:25,680
It has to be able to conduct electricity to some degree, otherwise it's going to build

483
00:39:25,681 --> 00:39:28,140
up a static charge.

484
00:39:28,141 --> 00:39:34,360
And the charges can be fairly large, which can create failures and can bring your satellite

485
00:39:34,361 --> 00:39:37,840
to its knees.

486
00:39:37,841 --> 00:39:43,540
Thanks in no small measure to space-age coatings, that is a rare occurrence.

487
00:39:43,541 --> 00:39:50,540
Back on Earth, the future of paint is also bright.

488
00:39:52,040 --> 00:39:56,000
Chemists continue to reduce the environmental impact of paint,

489
00:39:56,001 --> 00:39:59,940
while making it ever more durable and colorful.

490
00:39:59,941 --> 00:40:04,540
It's one of the things that spurs a technological advancement within the industry.

491
00:40:04,541 --> 00:40:10,240
You remove a lead, you remove a mercury, you've got to replace it with something.

492
00:40:10,241 --> 00:40:12,000
and oftentimes the replacements are superior.

493
00:40:13,420 --> 00:40:15,940
Without regulations, I may not even have a job.

494
00:40:17,580 --> 00:40:21,120
The primary concern today continues to be the element of paint

495
00:40:21,121 --> 00:40:25,160
that vanishes as soon as the paint dries, the solvent.

496
00:40:26,020 --> 00:40:30,520
The solvent portion of paints is compelling to me.

497
00:40:30,760 --> 00:40:32,780
It's really the part of the paint that's fugitive.

498
00:40:32,781 --> 00:40:35,520
It's in the wet product, but it's not in the dry product,

499
00:40:35,660 --> 00:40:37,120
so it's going to evaporate.

500
00:40:37,121 --> 00:40:44,060
The need for volatile solvents, those composed of liquids other than water, continues to

501
00:40:44,061 --> 00:40:52,400
decrease with the help of advanced technologies, like powder coatings.

502
00:40:52,401 --> 00:40:56,420
These tiny solid particles of paint contain no solvent at all.

503
00:40:56,421 --> 00:41:02,480
They're sprayed onto the substrate and heated to a liquid, which then hardens.

504
00:41:02,481 --> 00:41:10,520
A traditional enemy of paint, UV light, is used to flash cure paint like these samples,

505
00:41:10,521 --> 00:41:15,100
reducing the need for volatile drying solvents.

506
00:41:15,101 --> 00:41:19,720
Chemists are also tapping into literally green sources for the resin portion of paints.

507
00:41:19,721 --> 00:41:26,200
Now what we have over here is probably what you consider the next generation of coating

508
00:41:26,201 --> 00:41:27,600
technologies.

509
00:41:27,601 --> 00:41:35,000
This product in here is a synthetic resin that had its basis as a biomass, meaning that

510
00:41:35,001 --> 00:41:38,440
just two years ago this material in here was a plant.

511
00:41:38,441 --> 00:41:42,980
The benefit of that is for every pound of this resin you use, you take out about two

512
00:41:42,981 --> 00:41:45,840
and a half to three pounds of CO2 from the atmosphere.

513
00:41:45,841 --> 00:41:51,060
We reduce by an order of ten the amount of volatile organic components.

514
00:41:51,061 --> 00:41:55,320
That's the pollution part of a coating that goes into the atmosphere.

515
00:41:55,321 --> 00:41:58,580
Finally, the nice thing about this is that it's extremely,

516
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:00,700
the backbone of it is extremely durable.

517
00:42:01,000 --> 00:42:03,700
So it actually gives you better quality performance.

518
00:42:05,520 --> 00:42:10,140
There's still much work to be done in making paint a 100% clean technology.

519
00:42:11,660 --> 00:42:14,100
But the story of paint is far from over.

520
00:42:15,360 --> 00:42:18,000
The future, you could say, is a blank canvas.

521
00:42:19,660 --> 00:42:23,320
There's talk of putting microprocessors into paint films

522
00:42:23,321 --> 00:42:31,720
where these microprocessors in the paint are continuously informing, say, the bridge engineers

523
00:42:31,721 --> 00:42:36,620
as to what the wind shear is that the bridge is being exposed to.

524
00:42:37,540 --> 00:42:41,940
There has been some development of paints that can heal themselves,

525
00:42:42,720 --> 00:42:50,140
essentially using an encapsulation technology where you have small amounts of material in the paint

526
00:42:50,141 --> 00:42:57,220
that when the paint is ruptured, it releases a material which will move to the ruptured area.

527
00:42:59,940 --> 00:43:06,220
The Air Force has developed paints that change color in response to varying pressure levels during wind tunnel testing.

528
00:43:08,740 --> 00:43:15,460
And scientists at the University of Rhode Island have devised paints that change color to warn of dangerous overheating.

529
00:43:15,461 --> 00:43:24,360
No matter where paint takes us, it will go on making the world more colorful than our

530
00:43:24,361 --> 00:43:28,700
artistic ancestors could have dreamed.

531
00:43:28,701 --> 00:43:36,800
Meanwhile, the science behind paint will continue to evolve as quality-obsessed chemists and

532
00:43:36,801 --> 00:43:42,060
colorists try to make it better with each batch.

533
00:43:42,061 --> 00:43:46,560
You know, I talk to people and I say, I actually enjoy watching paint dry.

534
00:43:46,660 --> 00:43:47,360
I really do.

535
00:43:47,361 --> 00:43:49,120
I do it right here in this lab all the time.

536
00:43:49,540 --> 00:43:54,880
And you can see me actually standing there watching paint dry because it just fascinates

537
00:43:54,881 --> 00:43:55,581
me.

538
00:43:55,582 --> 00:43:57,500
It's such a complex thing.

